==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|distichon||a poem of two verses, a distich consisting of a hexameter and a pentameter}}, from {{der|en|grc|δίστιχον}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪstɪk/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s}}

# {{lb|en|prosody}} A [[couplet]], a two-line [[stanza]] making [[complete]] sense.
#: {{cot|en|monostich}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Christer Henriksén|title=A Commentary on Martial, Epigrams Book 9|year=2012|publisher=OUP Oxford|page=282|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TgPJfgToMpMC&lpg=PA282&dq=hl=es&pg=PA281#v=onepage&q&f=false|isbn=978-0-19-960631-3|passage=Through these '''distichs''' of increasing intensity and vagueness, the reader is brought to the riddle of the concluding '''distich''': as far as the speaker is concerned, the girl was ‘pure’, but she will not be if Aeschylus wants to receive the same service on a ‘bad condition’.}}
# Any couplet.

